{
  "_about": [
    "Shared vectors for which Jellyfin and Emby library views a scan reads, and what it asks them for. The contract is the web tuner's NostalgexJellyfinUrls.sectionType, .itemTypes and .hasAskedType (scripts/nostalgex-jellyfin-urls.js, point 12); the Apple TV's JellyfinAPIService.sectionType and .itemTypes, the Roku's NxMbItemTypesFor and the Android MediaBrowserLibrary.sectionType are to match it.",
    "Read by scripts/nostalgex-jellyfin-urls-smoke.mjs (npm run test:jellyfin-urls).",
    "`sections`: a view's CollectionType (null, or missing, is mixed content) gives a sectionType and the IncludeItemTypes to ask for. sectionType 'other' is a view with no videos in it: the scan never asks it for items, because Jellyfin's PlaylistsFolder answers any request with its playlists, whatever IncludeItemTypes says.",
    "`types`: a row the server sent, the types that were asked for, and whether the scan keeps the row. A row with no Type is kept.",
    "`views`: the request that lists a user's libraries, by backend, user id and the position of the INCLUDE LIBRARIES HIDDEN ON JELLYFIN switch (`includeHidden`: false is the default, off unless the viewer turns it on). `path` is what follows the server address, query string included, with the user id percent-encoded. Switch off, Jellyfin's request is the one it always was. Switch on, Jellyfin adds includeHidden=true: /UserViews leaves out a library the user hides from the home screen (Configuration.MyMediaExcludes) unless asked, and a library that is left out is never scanned. includeHidden only lifts that hiding; a library the user has no access to is still not in the answer. Emby has no such parameter, so its path is the same in both positions (on Emby 4.10.1.0 a user with libraries in MyMediaExcludes still got every library they may open)."
  ],
